    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth.jpg" alt="Bolesław III Wrymouth" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boleslaw_III_Wrymouth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Societas Scientiarum Plocensis (Towarzystwo Naukowe Płockie), based on the sketches of Jerzy Mazuś., CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, from a Slavic phrase meaning the land by the sea, was settled by West Slavic peoples who by the eleventh century lived in strongholds and trading towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolin">Wolin</a> and Kamień near the mouth of the Oder. Polish rulers had tried to subdue them since the tenth century, and around 1121–1122 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> conquered the lands around the lower Oder. The local ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartislaw-i-duke-of-pomerania">Wartislaw I</a>, the first historically attested member of the dynasty later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffins</a>, accepted Polish overlordship, paid tribute and agreed to the conversion of his people. Bolesław invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg">Otto of Bamberg</a>, who led missions in 1124 and 1128, baptising crowds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrzyce">Pyrzyce</a> and persuading an assembly of nobles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usedom">Usedom</a> to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>; the lives of Otto written by Ebo and Herbord describe pagan temples at Szczecin and Wolin being destroyed. Wartislaw was murdered around 1135, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Ratibor</a> I continued the line. A bishopric for Pomerania was founded at Wolin in 1140 and moved to Kamień (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamien-pomorski">Cammin</a>) in the 1170s. As Polish power fragmented after Bolesław’s death in 1138, the dukes turned towards the German princes: after defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and Danish forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-verchen">Verchen</a> in 1164 they submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, and in 1181 Bogislaw I received his duchy as an imperial fief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wolin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wolin.jpg" alt="Wolin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wolin">Wolin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zalewszczecinski.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Landsat, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomeranians-slavic-tribe">Slavic Pomeranians</a> lived from farming, fishing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and trade, and towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolin">Wolin</a> had been important Baltic markets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. From the late twelfth century the dukes, monasteries and nobles invited German settlers, and the Cistercian abbeys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolbacz-abbey">Kołbacz</a> (Kolbatz) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eldena-abbey">Eldena</a> helped clear and organise land. Barnim I, who ruled for over half a century until 1278, granted German town law to dozens of foundations, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a> in 1243, and the countryside was reorganised into villages of German and Slavic peasants paying fixed dues. Over the following centuries the population, especially in the west, became largely German-speaking, though Slavic speech survived longer in the east, where the Kashubian language still lives on near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. The coastal towns became members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, trading grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, herring and beer across the Baltic; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> was one of the leading Hanse cities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stralsund-1370">Peace of Stralsund</a> of 1370 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> marked the height of the League’s power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a>’s university, founded in 1456 by the burgomaster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-rubenow">Heinrich Rubenow</a> with the support of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartislaw-ix-duke-of-pomerania">Wartislaw IX</a>, was among the oldest in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After 1534 the duchy was Lutheran, and the Pomeranian church order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a> shaped its religious life, schools and poor relief.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/absalon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/absalon.jpg" alt="Absalon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Absalons_ligsten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was never a great military power, and much of its history was spent defending itself against stronger neighbours. In the twelfth century the dukes fought Polish rulers, Danish kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> princes; Danish expeditions under Valdemar I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a> devastated the coast and destroyed the temple fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> on Rügen in 1168. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> made the Pomeranian dukes their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> from 1185 until their defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bornhoved">Battle of Bornhöved</a> in 1227. The most persistent threat came from the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, who in 1231 obtained from Emperor Frederick II a claim to overlordship and took territory such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uckermark">Uckermark</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-landin">Treaty of Landin</a> in 1250. Wars with Brandenburg over that claim recurred until the fifteenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a> fought them again in the 1470s before settling the question by treaty. The Hanseatic towns maintained their own forces and fleets and often pursued their own policies. The duchy’s weakness was exposed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War: in 1627 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a> had to accept imperial troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a>, who besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> in 1628 and failed to take it after the town received Danish and Swedish help. In 1630 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usedom">Usedom</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a> and forced the duke into an alliance, and Pomerania became a battlefield and supply base for the rest of the war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x.jpg" alt="Bogislaw X" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bogislaw_X_von_Pommern.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unbekannter Meister, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin</a> dukes ruled as hereditary princes, but the dynasty often divided its lands among sons, creating separate lines whose names reflected their main seats. The most important partition, in 1295, created the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-stettin">Pomerania-Stettin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-wolgast">Pomerania-Wolgast</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolgast">Wolgast</a> gained the Principality of Rügen in 1325 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> was confirmed as an immediate imperial fief, free of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> overlordship, in 1338. Further divisions followed, including the lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a> and Barth. The duchy was a fief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, though Brandenburg contested the dukes’ imperial status for generations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a> reunited all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1478 and strengthened ducal government, reforming finances, appointing trained officials and limiting the independence of nobles and towns. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pyritz">Treaty of Pyritz</a> in 1493 Brandenburg gave up its claim to overlordship in return for a right of succession should the Griffins die out, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-grimnitz">Treaty of Grimnitz</a> in 1529. In the sixteenth century the estates of the duchy, the nobility, the towns and the clergy, met in diets and gained a strong voice in taxation; it was a diet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebiatow">Treptow</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rega">Rega</a> in 1534 that adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. In 1532 the duchy was again divided between Stettin and Wolgast, and it was reunited only in 1625 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stralsund"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stralsund.jpg" alt="Stralsund" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WLM_-_2020_-_Stralsund_-_St._Nikolai_Kirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Moahim, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was one of the clearest examples of the medieval transformation of the southern Baltic, in which Slavic principalities adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, German law and settlement, and urban trade while keeping their native dynasties. Its Christianisation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg">Otto of Bamberg</a>, later venerated as the Apostle of Pomerania, became a model case of missionary work in the region. Its towns helped make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> the dominant force in Baltic commerce, and their brick Gothic churches and town halls still define the landscape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>. One <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin</a> prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>, a great-nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I of Denmark</a>, was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1397 at the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>; deposed in the late 1430s, he ended his life ruling a small Pomeranian duchy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Darłowo</a> (Rügenwalde). The court culture of the last Griffins is known through objects such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croy-tapestry">Croÿ Tapestry</a> of 1554, showing the dynasty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, and the Pomeranian Art Cabinet made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> for Duke Philip II, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eilhard-lubinus">Eilhard Lubinus</a>’s great map of the duchy of 1618. The division of the duchy in 1648 shaped the borders of Swedish and Brandenburg-Prussian Pomerania for centuries, and the Griffin emblem still appears in regional heraldry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv.jpg" alt="Bogislaw XIV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barnim_VI_-_Bogus%C5%82aw_XIV_(ca_1635).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy did not fall to conquest so much as end with its dynasty. By the early seventeenth century the many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin</a> princes had few sons, and when the brothers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a> died one after another he became the only ruler of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1625, with no heir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">The Thirty</a> Years’ War then overwhelmed the duchy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Wallenstein</a>’s imperial army occupied it in 1627 and, after the Swedish landing of 1630, Bogislaw signed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> that effectively placed Pomerania under Swedish military control. Armies of both sides requisitioned food and quarters, and war, plague and famine reduced the population drastically, by some estimates by more than half in many districts. Bogislaw suffered a stroke in 1633 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a> in March 1637, ending the male line of the Griffins after some five centuries. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pyritz">Treaty of Pyritz</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> should have inherited, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> would not give up its occupation, and the duke was not buried until 1654. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648 divided the duchy: Sweden kept Western Pomerania with Szczecin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usedom">Usedom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolin">Wolin</a>, and Brandenburg received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/further-pomerania">Farther Pomerania</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1653">Treaty of Stettin</a> of 1653 fixed the border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> gained the southern part of Swedish Pomerania, with Szczecin, in 1720 and the rest in 1815, and after 1945 most of the old duchy became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> shows how a Slavic Baltic principality was Christianised, settled by Germans and drawn into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the Hanseatic world while keeping its native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin dynasty</a> for five centuries, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War and the end of the line divided it between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>.</em></p>

